1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.602
Welcome to Semantic Caching for AI Agents

2
00:00:02.602 --> 00:00:04.471
built in partnership with Redis,

3
00:00:04.471 --> 00:00:06.172
taught by Tyler Hutcherson,

4
00:00:06.172 --> 00:00:08.775
who is applied AI engineering lead

5
00:00:08.775 --> 00:00:10.377
and Iliya Zhechev,

6
00:00:10.410 --> 00:00:13.213
who is senior research engineer at Redis.

7
00:00:13.913 --> 00:00:16.916
In this course, you'll learn how to make your AI agents faster

8
00:00:16.916 --> 00:00:19.919
and more cost-effective by adding a semantic cache.

9
00:00:20.420 --> 00:00:23.223
In many projects, inference costs and latency

10
00:00:23.223 --> 00:00:25.425
affect your ability to scale an application.

11
00:00:25.925 --> 00:00:27.894
Traditional input-output caches that work when

12
00:00:27.894 --> 00:00:30.196
the input text is exactly the same

13
00:00:30.196 --> 00:00:31.898
can help in some cases,

14
00:00:32.399 --> 00:00:35.201
but if someone asks, how can I get a refund?

15
00:00:35.201 --> 00:00:37.604
and another person asks, I want my money back.

16
00:00:37.904 --> 00:00:41.674
a normal exact match cache sees them as completely different.

17
00:00:42.275 --> 00:00:44.878
Semantic caching on the other hand looks at meaning.

18
00:00:44.878 --> 00:00:47.881
It uses embeddings to measure how similar two questions are

19
00:00:47.881 --> 00:00:50.383
in this meaning space.

20
00:00:50.383 --> 00:00:54.387
And if they're semantically similar, then we can reuse the model's response

21
00:00:54.387 --> 00:00:57.791
to an old answer instead of calling the model again. Thanks, Andrew.

22
00:00:57.991 --> 00:00:59.993
You'll first build a cache from scratch

23
00:01:00.026 --> 00:01:03.396
to learn under the hood of semantic caching piece by piece.

24
00:01:03.797 --> 00:01:06.099
You will create embeddings, compare distances,

25
00:01:06.299 --> 00:01:08.034
and set a threshold to decide

26
00:01:08.034 --> 00:01:10.070
when two queries are semantically similar enough.

27
00:01:10.336 --> 00:01:13.039
Then we'll move it into implementing our semantic cache

28
00:01:13.239 --> 00:01:15.308
using Redis as open source SDK.

29
00:01:15.809 --> 00:01:18.311
This will make your cache closer to a production deployment

30
00:01:18.411 --> 00:01:21.114
as your cache will now have features like time to live

31
00:01:21.214 --> 00:01:23.116
to keep your cache fresh and small,

32
00:01:23.416 --> 00:01:26.219
and even separate caches for different users or teams or tenants.

33
00:01:26.719 --> 00:01:29.222
We'll also use our open weight embedding model,

34
00:01:29.222 --> 00:01:31.024
fine-tuned for cache accuracy.

35
00:01:31.224 --> 00:01:32.926
Once you have a working cache,

36
00:01:32.926 --> 00:01:34.527
we'll measure how well it performs.

37
00:01:34.727 --> 00:01:36.930
We'll look at hit rate, precision, and recall.

38
00:01:37.397 --> 00:01:40.400
These metrics will show how often your cache helps

39
00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:42.368
and how often it is correct.

40
00:01:42.402 --> 00:01:44.871
You'll visualize them in a confusion matrix

41
00:01:44.904 --> 00:01:47.373
and you'll see how changing the similarity threshold

42
00:01:47.407 --> 00:01:49.876
will shift the balance between precision and recall.

43
00:01:50.443 --> 00:01:52.445
We'll also look at latency

44
00:01:52.645 --> 00:01:54.948
and we'll see how a few hits

45
00:01:55.048 --> 00:01:56.950
quickly add up to a big time saving.

46
00:01:57.150 --> 00:01:59.352
After measuring the effectiveness of your cache,

47
00:01:59.452 --> 00:02:01.454
you'll learn four methods for enhancing it.

48
00:02:01.654 --> 00:02:03.356
You'll optimize the threshold

49
00:02:03.456 --> 00:02:05.959
and we'll use a cross-encoder for better re-ranking.

50
00:02:06.059 --> 00:02:08.161
And even a small LLM check that can confirm

51
00:02:08.261 --> 00:02:10.263
whether two questions mean the same thing.

52
00:02:10.897 --> 00:02:13.500
We also add fuzzy matching to handle simple typos

53
00:02:13.700 --> 00:02:17.003
that frequently happen when users ask questions.

54
00:02:17.103 --> 00:02:19.606
Finally, we'll connect it all inside an AI agent.

55
00:02:19.906 --> 00:02:22.609
The agent breaks a big question into smaller parts

56
00:02:22.709 --> 00:02:24.544
and checks the cache for each one and

57
00:02:24.544 --> 00:02:26.412
only calls the LLM when it needs to.

58
00:02:26.946 --> 00:02:29.716
This means every new user or slightly different phrasing

59
00:02:29.716 --> 00:02:31.718
benefits from what the system already knows.

60
00:02:32.018 --> 00:02:34.020
Over time, as the cache warms up,

61
00:02:34.420 --> 00:02:36.222
model calls drop and responses feel

62
00:02:36.222 --> 00:02:38.324
just as good but arrive much faster.

63
00:02:38.725 --> 00:02:40.727
Many people have worked to create this course.

64
00:02:41.127 --> 00:02:42.729
I'd like to thank from Redis,

65
00:02:42.929 --> 00:02:47.333
the applied AI, AI research, product, and education teams.

66
00:02:47.534 --> 00:02:51.337
From DeepLearning.AI, Esmaeil Gargari also contributed to this course.

67
00:02:51.738 --> 00:02:54.641
The first lesson will be an overview of semantic caching.

68
00:02:55.108 --> 00:02:57.577
You will also learn about a real use case

69
00:02:57.710 --> 00:02:59.879
where Walmart published techniques

70
00:03:00.079 --> 00:03:02.882
to improve their production caching system.

71
00:03:03.283 --> 00:03:05.885
So, let's go into the next video and get started.
